Universal Settlement Layer: the format the hot side writes and the cold side
reads, one file per media on the stick. The envelope carries no signature -
signing stays with the cold device, and it is deliberately not implemented
here, because a std-only crate must not invent crypto it cannot be audited
for. What this crate does guarantee is the byte contract: entries hashed in
order into a seal (muhur's recomputation mechanism, not a stored trust), so
a media edited in transit - or half-written by a flaky device - fails the
reader's recompute before a human ever reads an amount off it.

Design decisions recorded at their seam:
- Amount is the layer's own unit: canonical M.mm, MINOR_PER_MAJOR=100 as
  this layer's constant. No caller's currency is modeled; each side maps at
  its own boundary and nothing is silently converted - a hot side that meant
  satoshis and a cold side that read lira is the bug this spelling refuses.
- Payout lines cannot contain tab, quote, backslash, control characters,
  double spaces, or leading/trailing spaces; the media grammar is
  space-tokenized and a field that could embed the separators could rewrite
  its own line. The refusal lives in the constructor, so an illegal Payout
  cannot exist to be sealed.
- Duplicate payout lines (same address, amount, memo) are refused as
  assembly mistakes, not treated as intentional double payments.
- The window is enforced where it is set, not only at check: an author can
  hold a half-built envelope but cannot seal one that ends where it begins.
  check() repeats the rule so reader and writer enforce exactly the same one.
- from_media replays the chain through Indexed, so the index the seal covers
  is checked against the chain, not trusted beside it (muhur's own rule:
  a second index can lie in a second way).
- to_media seals the exact Vec it built, not a re-derived copy: what hashes
  and what lands in the file are the same strings.

muhur gains Indexed::finalize (the seal is reached through the index that
points at it) and loses chain_mut in the same step - handing out &mut Chain
let a caller append after sealing and move the tip under the index's feet;
its only user in the tree was the test that is now written through the
proper door. This retires one baseline entry for good (41 to 40): deleted
surface, not a widened exemption.

lubot usl make|check is the first consumer: flags parse, per-payout pairing
(--to/--amount counts must match, --memo per line or none), the written
path prints the sealed total, check re-derives and echoes every payout line
- which is also what wires Payout::to/memo/amount, so the crate ships with
zero new baseline entries.

CLI: usage block, dispatch arm, cmd_usl; Cargo.toml: crate member, cli dep
lubot-usl, usl dep lubot-muhur (std-only, leaf); docs/CRATES.md: header
twelve to thirteen, usl row with its own failure column; README: 328 to 334
